## Keep the feature in one place

This is reference code for the separate full-stack application. The handbook lab uses ordinary React state to simulate the contract; it does not install a query library or contact Django.

```text
src/features/reports/
  api/reports.ts             wire types and transport
  hooks/useReports.ts       server-state identity and refresh
  hooks/useReportsController.ts  form, selection, uncertain commands
  pages/ReportsPage.tsx      compose the workspace
  parts/ReportsWorkspace.tsx explicit props and visual states
  index.ts                  public feature exports
```

Use the application's existing auth and organization providers. An access token passed into these examples must come from that auth boundary; do not hard-code it or store it in a tutorial file.

## Type and capture the request

```typescript
// api/reports.ts — Transport without component state.
export type Scope = { orgSlug: string; userId: string; accessToken: string };
export type Report = {
  id: string; title: string; status: 'draft'; createdAt: string;
};
export type CreateReport = { title: string; creationKey: string };
export type ReportPage = {
  count: number; next: string | null; previous: string | null; results: Report[];
};
export class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(public status: number, public payload: unknown) {
    super(`Reports request failed (${status})`);
  }
}
const API = '/api/v1/reports/';
async function request<T>(scope: Scope, path: string, init: RequestInit = {}): Promise<T> {
  const response = await fetch(path, {
    ...init,
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      Authorization: `Bearer ${scope.accessToken}`,
      'X-Org-Slug': scope.orgSlug,
    },
  });
  const payload: unknown = await response.json();
  if (!response.ok) throw new ApiError(response.status, payload);
  return payload as T; // Use runtime schema validation at an untrusted boundary.
}
export const reportsApi = {
  list: (scope: Scope, signal?: AbortSignal) =>
    request<ReportPage>(scope, `${API}?limit=20&offset=0`, { signal }),
  detail: (scope: Scope, id: string, signal?: AbortSignal) =>
    request<Report>(scope, `${API}${encodeURIComponent(id)}/`, { signal }),
  create: (scope: Scope, input: CreateReport) =>
    request<Report>(scope, API, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(input) }),
};
```

Mount these hooks under the application’s `QueryClientProvider`. A production transport must also handle non-JSON proxy errors, token refresh, timeout/cancellation policy, and an explicit API base URL where the frontend and backend use different origins. Do not turn a JSON parse error into an automatic new create command. TypeScript annotations alone do not validate a response at runtime.

## Give snapshots a complete identity

```typescript
// hooks/useReports.ts — TanStack Query v5 reference integration.
import { useMutation, useQuery, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { reportsApi, type Scope, type CreateReport } from '../api/reports';
export const reportKeys = {
  scope: (scope: Scope) => ['org', scope.orgSlug, 'user', scope.userId, 'reports'] as const,
  list: (scope: Scope) => [...reportKeys.scope(scope), 'list', { limit: 20, offset: 0 }] as const,
  detail: (scope: Scope, id: string) => [...reportKeys.scope(scope), 'detail', id] as const,
};
export function useReports(scope: Scope) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: reportKeys.list(scope),
    queryFn: ({ signal }) => reportsApi.list(scope, signal),
  });
}
export function useReportDetail(scope: Scope, id: string | null) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: reportKeys.detail(scope, id ?? "none"),
    queryFn: ({ signal }) => reportsApi.detail(scope, id!, signal),
    enabled: id !== null,
  });
}
export function useCreateReport() {
  const cache = useQueryClient();
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: ({ scope, input }: { scope: Scope; input: CreateReport }) =>
      reportsApi.create(scope, input),
    retry: false,
    onSuccess: (_report, variables) => {
      // Refresh failures belong to query state, not the create result.
      void cache.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: reportKeys.scope(variables.scope) });
    },
  });
}
```

The mutation receives the captured scope, so a delayed response cannot invalidate a newly selected tenant by accident. Query identity includes tenant, actor, and list inputs. Clear the query cache at logout and identity change; revalidate on membership changes. Never use the access token itself as a cache key. TanStack documents query keys as the identity of cached data. [Query key guide](https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/guides/query-keys).

## Let the controller own recovery

The controller combines query state, selected report, and the current command. Use a keyed feature boundary such as `<ReportsPage key={`${orgSlug}:${userId}`} scope={scope} />` to remount local state on scope changes. On logout, remove that boundary and clear cached protected data.

```typescript
// hooks/useReportsController.ts — Retain one command until its outcome is known.
import { useState } from 'react';
import { ApiError, type Scope, type CreateReport } from '../api/reports';
import { useReports, useCreateReport } from './useReports';
export function useReportsController(scope: Scope) {
  const query = useReports(scope);
  const create = useCreateReport();
  const [title, setTitle] = useState('');
  const [pending, setPending] = useState<CreateReport | null>(null);
  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');
  async function submit() {
    const command = pending ?? { title: title.trim(), creationKey: crypto.randomUUID() };
    setPending(command);
    try {
      await create.mutateAsync({ scope, input: command });
      setPending(null); setTitle(''); setMessage('Draft created or recovered.');
    } catch (error) {
      const rejected = error instanceof ApiError && [400, 401, 403, 404, 409].includes(error.status);
      if (rejected) setPending(null);
      setMessage(rejected ? 'Request rejected. Resolve the error before creating again.'
        : 'Outcome unknown. Retry this same command before editing.');
    }
  }
  return { query, title, setTitle, submit, message,
    busy: create.isPending, uncertain: pending !== null };
}
```

Disable concurrent submit while `busy`. Disable title edits while a command is uncertain and offer a clearly named “Retry same request” action. Surface field errors from 400 rather than only the abbreviated message above. A 409 is a conflict to reconcile, not permission to automatically generate a replacement key. The page below owns selected-detail composition; scope remounting clears that selection.

## Compose the page without hiding states

```tsx
// pages/ReportsPage.tsx — Compose controller output into explicit visual states.
import { useState } from 'react';
import { type Scope } from '../api/reports';
import { useReportDetail } from '../hooks/useReports';
import { useReportsController } from '../hooks/useReportsController';
import { ReportsWorkspace } from '../parts/ReportsWorkspace';
export function ReportsPage({ scope }: { scope: Scope }) {
  const c = useReportsController(scope);
  const [selectedId, setSelectedId] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const detail = useReportDetail(scope, selectedId);
  return <ReportsWorkspace
    reports={c.query.data?.results ?? []}
    loading={c.query.isPending} failed={c.query.isError}
    title={c.title} onTitleChange={c.setTitle} onSubmit={c.submit}
    busy={c.busy} uncertain={c.uncertain} message={c.message}
    selectedId={selectedId} onSelect={setSelectedId}
    detail={detail.data} detailPending={detail.isFetching} detailFailed={detail.isError}
  />;
}
```

```tsx
// parts/ReportsWorkspace.tsx — Render explicit states without fetching competing data.
import { type Report } from '../api/reports';
type Props = {
  reports: Report[]; loading: boolean; failed: boolean;
  title: string; onTitleChange: (value: string) => void;
  onSubmit: () => Promise<void>; busy: boolean; uncertain: boolean; message: string;
  selectedId: string | null; onSelect: (id: string) => void;
  detail?: Report; detailPending: boolean; detailFailed: boolean;
};
export function ReportsWorkspace(p: Props) {
  return <section aria-label="Reports workspace">
    <h1>Reports</h1>
    <form onSubmit={event => { event.preventDefault(); void p.onSubmit(); }}>
      <label>Title<input value={p.title} maxLength={120}
        disabled={p.busy || p.uncertain}
        onChange={event => p.onTitleChange(event.target.value)}/></label>
      <button disabled={p.busy || (!p.uncertain && !p.title.trim())}>
        {p.busy ? 'Submitting…' : p.uncertain ? 'Retry same request' : 'Create draft'}
      </button>
    </form>
    <p role="status">{p.message}</p>
    {p.loading ? <p>Loading reports…</p> : p.failed ?
      <p role="alert">Reports could not be loaded. Recheck access and retry the read.</p> :
      p.reports.length === 0 ? <p>No reports in this workspace.</p> :
      <ul>{p.reports.map(report => <li key={report.id}>
        <button onClick={() => p.onSelect(report.id)}>{report.title}</button>
      </li>)}</ul>}
    {p.selectedId && <aside aria-label="Report detail">
      {p.detailPending ? <p>Loading detail…</p> : p.detailFailed ?
        <p role="alert">This report is unavailable in the current scope.</p> :
        p.detail && <><h2>{p.detail.title}</h2><p>Status: {p.detail.status}</p></>}
    </aside>}
  </section>;
}
```

```typescript
// index.ts — Expose the feature page at its module boundary.
export { ReportsPage } from './pages/ReportsPage';
```

The presentational workspace takes these props. Show loading, forbidden/error, empty, ready, submitting, and uncertain outcomes distinctly. The runnable [lab](/lab/reports) provides an intentionally compact UI for the command and tenant exercises; it does not claim to implement this TanStack reference integration.

## Checkpoint

Can a delayed North mutation affect the South interface? Can a retry silently obtain a new creation key? Can old detail survive a scope change? Continue to [verification and failure drills](/guide/reports-verification).

## Start with a read-only connection

Use the [Tasks API](/guide/django-first-api) and the [frontend layout](/guide/frontend-folder-structure). The code below is reference integration for a separate React project with Axios and TanStack Query v5 installed. Login remains the local Django browser login for this exercise.

For local development, proxy API requests through Vite so the browser sees one origin. Log in through the frontend's `/api-auth/login/` route, then return to the app. Keep `localhost` versus `127.0.0.1` consistent in the browser.

```typescript
// frontend/vite.config.ts — Proxy the learning API and its login during development.
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react()],
  server: { proxy: {
    '/api/': 'http://127.0.0.1:8000',
    '/api-auth/': 'http://127.0.0.1:8000',
  } },
});
```

This proxy exists during development. Production needs deliberate routing or a cross-origin policy; static hosting cannot execute Django.

## Transport belongs in the client

```typescript
// frontend/src/api/client.ts — Centralize the same-origin learning transport.
import axios from 'axios';
export const api = axios.create({ baseURL: '/api/', timeout: 15_000 });
```

For separate-origin deployments, choose the API URL through public environment configuration. For cookie authentication, configure credentials, trusted origins, secure cookie policy and CSRF together. Do not simply enable credentials and wildcard CORS. This read example does not implement a mutation or its required CSRF handling.

```typescript
// frontend/src/features/tasks/api.ts — Define the starter's unpaginated list contract.
import { api } from '../../api/client';
export type Task = { id: number; title: string; completed: boolean; created_at: string };
export async function listTasks(signal?: AbortSignal): Promise<Task[]> {
  const response = await api.get<Task[]>('tasks/', { signal });
  return response.data;
}
```

## Server state belongs in TanStack Query

```typescript
// frontend/src/features/tasks/queries.ts — Scope snapshots to the current identity.
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import axios from 'axios';
import { listTasks } from './api';
export const taskKeys = { list: (userId: string) => ['users', userId, 'tasks'] as const };
export function useTasks(userId: string) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: taskKeys.list(userId),
    queryFn: ({ signal }) => listTasks(signal),
    enabled: Boolean(userId),
    retry: (count, error) => {
      if (axios.isAxiosError(error) && [401, 403].includes(error.response?.status ?? 0)) return false;
      return count < 2;
    },
  });
}
```

Get `userId` from the application's verified session state, not a hardcoded production identity. For organization data, include the organization ID and normalized filters in the key too. Cache keys partition snapshots; the API still checks access. Axios supports [AbortController cancellation](https://axios-http.com/docs/cancellation), allowing the query signal to reach the request.

```tsx
// frontend/src/app/providers.tsx — Create one query client for this browser application.
import type { ReactNode } from 'react';
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';
export const queryClient = new QueryClient();
export function AppProviders({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>{children}</QueryClientProvider>;
}
```

Wrap `<App />` with `<AppProviders>` in `main.tsx`. A task page consumes `useTasks(userId)` and renders pending, error, empty and success states. Keep form drafts and dialog state in React.

## Plan writes and session changes

On a successful task mutation, invalidate `taskKeys.list(userId)` using the authoritative response and current scope. Do not automatically retry a non-idempotent write. On logout or identity change, cancel in-flight private queries and clear their cache before displaying the next session. An organization switch needs new keys and current authorization, not a recycled list from the previous tenant.

The [TanStack Query quickstart](https://tanstack.com/query/latest/docs/framework/react/quick-start) documents provider, query and mutation APIs. Verify actual login, session expiry, failed fetches and switching identities in your integration; these snippets alone do not provide an authentication system.

## When the frontend and API have different origins

Follow the dedicated [CORS chapter](/guide/django-cors) for direct local requests, deployed origin allowlists, preflight, credentials and CSRF. Keep the development proxy and production routing decisions explicit.

## Before you start

The application must already resolve a session with explicit loading, signed-in and signed-out states. This example uses React Router's `Navigate` and `Outlet`. It does not implement token verification or login.

## File and integration excerpt

```tsx
// frontend/src/app/RequireSession.tsx — Avoid rendering private screens before session resolution.
import { Navigate, Outlet, useLocation } from 'react-router';
type SessionState = 'loading' | 'signed-in' | 'signed-out' | 'unavailable';
export function RequireSession({ state }: { state: SessionState }) {
  const location = useLocation();
  if (state === 'loading') return <p role="status">Checking your session…</p>;
  if (state === 'unavailable') return <p role="alert">Session service unavailable. Try again.</p>;
  if (state === 'signed-out') return <Navigate to="/login" replace state={{ from: location.pathname }} />;
  return <Outlet />;
}
```

Nest private routes beneath this element and pass the real provider's session state. Keep `/login` outside the protected branch. Only accept safe internal return paths after login; do not redirect to arbitrary submitted URLs.

## Verify both boundaries

Check a direct private URL, reload during session restoration, expired login, an unavailable provider and logout. Clear private query data when the identity ends or changes. Call the underlying endpoint directly without credentials and as an unauthorized user: the API must deny access even when the route guard is bypassed.

A React route guard improves navigation. The [authentication options](/guide/authentication-options) and [Gait walkthrough](/guide/gait-auth-walkthrough) explain the actual identity and permission boundaries.

## Before you start

Your backend must implement an authenticated file endpoint that resolves the document through the caller's authorized scope. The following browser excerpt assumes that endpoint streams approved bytes. Do not replace it with a public storage URL.

## File and integration excerpt

```typescript
// frontend/src/features/documents/api.ts — Retrieve an authorized PDF preview through the API.
import { api } from '../../api/client';
export async function loadDocumentPreview(documentId: string, signal?: AbortSignal) {
  const response = await api.get<Blob>(`documents/${encodeURIComponent(documentId)}/file/`, {
    responseType: 'blob', signal,
  });
  if (response.data.type.split(';')[0] !== 'application/pdf') {
    throw new Error('Expected a PDF preview');
  }
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(response.data);
  return { url, dispose: () => URL.revokeObjectURL(url) };
}
```

Adapt the URL to your actual API contract. The caller owns `dispose`: call it when replacing a preview, closing it, switching organizations or unmounting. Cancel outstanding loads, and dispose a late result if its preview owner has already closed. Impose server-side type and size limits; MIME metadata alone does not establish file safety.

## Server checklist

Verify identity, active membership, document permission and scoped record lookup before opening storage. Keep the bucket private. If using signed URLs instead of streaming, define their lifetime and revocation limitations explicitly; they are a different delivery contract.

## Verify

Test a foreign document ID, removed membership, missing storage object, expired credentials, close during loading and repeated preview opening. Confirm private data and Blob URLs are cleared on a workspace change. Follow the [document lifecycle](/guide/private-document-lifecycle) for the full server workflow.

## Before you start

Implement an authenticated, authorized socket endpoint using the [WebSocket contract](/guide/websocket-design) and [Channels boundary](/guide/django-channels). The excerpt assumes cookie-session authentication has already been deliberately configured. It does not attach bearer credentials or implement reconnects.

## File and integration excerpt

```typescript
// frontend/src/features/tasks/live.ts — Observe a defined event and return connection cleanup.
import type { QueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';
export function observeTaskChanges(url: string, userId: string, queryClient: QueryClient) {
  const socket = new WebSocket(url);
  const onMessage = (event: MessageEvent) => {
    if (typeof event.data !== 'string') return;
    try {
      const message: unknown = JSON.parse(event.data);
      if (message && typeof message === 'object' && 'type' in message && message.type === 'tasks.changed') {
        void queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['users', userId, 'tasks'] });
      }
    } catch { /* Malformed events do not update application state. */ }
  };
  socket.addEventListener('message', onMessage);
  return () => {
    socket.removeEventListener('message', onMessage);
    socket.close(1000, 'Leaving task view');
  };
}
```

Call from a feature effect and return its cleanup; use the verified current user ID and a configured `wss://` URL in production. Scope the server subscription to that user. For tenant data, include the organization in both the subscription and query keys. Coalesce or rate-limit frequent invalidations in a busy stream.

## Verify

Check authorized connection, rejected origin, foreign subscription, malformed event, unmount, logout and dropped connection. This minimal excerpt intentionally needs separate connection-status and reconnect/snapshot handling before a resilient production experience. Do not silently present a disconnected stream as healthy.

## Before you start

Use the Tasks API and [Axios/Query connection](/guide/react-api-connection). Configure the authentication and CSRF handling required by your API before using POST; the read-only client example alone does not implement session writes.

## File and integration excerpt

```typescript
// frontend/src/features/tasks/useCreateTask.ts — Refresh this user's list after a confirmed write.
import { useMutation, useQueryClient } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { api } from '../../api/client';
import type { Task } from './api';
import { taskKeys } from './queries';
export function useCreateTask(userId: string) {
  const queryClient = useQueryClient();
  return useMutation({
    mutationFn: async (title: string) => {
      const response = await api.post<Task>('tasks/', { title });
      return response.data;
    },
    onSuccess: async () => {
      await queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: taskKeys.list(userId) });
    },
    retry: false,
  });
}
```

Use inside a signed-in screen and submit through its mutation state. Preserve a useful form draft on failure and prevent accidental repeat submissions. A lost response can follow a committed write, so disabling retries is not an idempotency guarantee. Commands that must be safely replayed need a backend idempotency contract.

## Verify

Check successful creation, validation errors, CSRF/authentication rejection, a response lost after commit and logout while the mutation is pending. On identity changes, ensure no late callback repopulates the next user's private view. For organization data, partition all affected keys by organization and invalidate derived consumers deliberately.

The [frontend state guide](/guide/frontend-state-contracts) explains downstream consumers and why a global refetch is usually a poor substitute for known dependencies.
